### FEATURE_DISCOVERY — the work a business does, discovered from what its people said
###
### Every request here is a real one against a running instance. Sign in first: the session cookie and
### the cross-site token are what the chain expects. The demo backend runs on 8081 — Tomcat9 owns 8080
### on this machine.
###
### **Two endpoints, and between them they are the entire input to this zone.** Everything downstream —
### threading, clocks, fingerprints, clustering, discovered patterns — is computed from what these two
### produce, and none of it asks anybody anything.
###
### **Nothing here can reach a task.** DECISION-DISCOVERY-ZONE-01 draws two zones and lets exactly one
### thing cross, one way: an approved template. A node never becomes a task. There is no route from
### these packages to TASK's write surface and `NoRouteFromDiscoveryToATaskTest` asserts its absence
### rather than leaving it as an intention — so no request you can write in this file will create one.
###
### Seed the workspace in discovery mode first, or there will be no messages to mark:
###   ./mvnw spring-boot:run -Dspring-boot.run.arguments="--discovery"
### That mode seeds seven people, seventeen conversations and 656 messages, and **zero task templates,
### zero process templates, zero tasks and zero runs**. That emptiness is the point: a zone that claims
### to find process structure nobody typed cannot be demonstrated next to a library somebody typed.

@host = http://localhost:8081
@owner = maria@atelier.ro
@manager = ionut@atelier.ro
@employee = andrei@atelier.ro
@password = a-long-enough-passphrase

### Copy real identifiers out of the two GETs below into these three.
@messageId = 00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000
@secondMessageId = 00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000
@jobId = 00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000
@secondJobId = 00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000
@performerId = 00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000
@conversationId = 00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000
@nodeId = 00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000

### Sign in as the manager who runs the client work.
POST {{host}}/api/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "{{manager}}",
  "password": "{{password}}"
}

### Your own conversations. Take an id from here — there is no route that lists anybody else's.
GET {{host}}/api/conversations

### The messages in one of them. Every one of these is markable by you and by nobody outside it.
GET {{host}}/api/conversations/00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000/messages

### Who is in the workspace, for the performer id below.
GET {{host}}/api/workspace/members

###############################################################################
### DISCOVERY-OPEN-JOB-01 — the one click nothing can infer
###############################################################################

### 201. The engagement, its first unit of work, and the thread that work opened.
###
### **This is the single most valuable click in the product.** Recovering which instance an event
### belongs to is the hardest known problem in process mining and the literature's heuristics for doing
### it from an unlabelled log are unreliable. One tap settles it, from the person who actually knows.
###
### The message becomes the engagement's first unit of work — STANDALONE, of kind JOB_START, kept for
### the person who marked it. Pasting a brief is somebody recording that an engagement now exists; it is
### not a request made of another person, and reading it as one would invent a handover that never
### happened and put a phantom edge at the start of every engagement in the workspace.
###
### Copy `jobId` out of the response into the variable above.
POST {{host}}/api/discovery/jobs
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "messageId": "{{messageId}}",
  "name": "Aurora Coffee — autumn campaign",
  "projectLabel": "Autumn campaign"
}

### S3 — `projectLabel` is OPTIONAL, and omitting it is an ordinary path rather than a degraded one.
###
### PRINCIPLE-NEVER-BLOCK: an employee opens an engagement in one click and a manager names the project
### afterwards. `Job.tracks` is settable after opening for exactly that reason.
###
### **It is part of the bracket address (D1), and it is applied BEFORE the boundary's address is
### composed.** An address is frozen at open, so setting it afterwards would key the container on a null
### project while every bracket opened a second later keys on the real one — two addresses for one
### engagement, and nothing on any screen saying so. A later change re-keys nothing that already exists;
### it decides what NEW brackets key on.
POST {{host}}/api/discovery/jobs
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

{
  "messageId": "{{messageId}}",
  "name": "Aurora Coffee — autumn campaign"
}

### 422 MESSAGE_NOT_MARKABLE, for a message that does not exist.
###
### **The same answer you get for a message that is not yours, and for one that was deleted.** Three
### distinguishable refusals would let somebody learn which message identifiers exist by watching which
### one comes back. Try it as the employee below and compare: identical.
POST {{host}}/api/discovery/jobs
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "messageId": "11111111-1111-1111-1111-111111111111",
  "name": "An engagement that will not be created"
}

###############################################################################
### DISCOVERY-MARK-MESSAGE-01 — the circle
###############################################################################

### 201. A request of a colleague. The thread comes back and you were asked nothing to get it.
###
### **The direction is yours, and it is never read out of the words.** Classifying by content is refused
### outright: a wrong direction does not merely mislabel, it fabricates a duration by pairing a
### completion with a request that was never its own — and because the same mistake repeats on every
### client, that false edge accumulates evidence rather than washing out.
POST {{host}}/api/discovery/nodes
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "messageId": "{{secondMessageId}}",
  "jobId": "{{jobId}}",
  "direction": "REQUEST",
  "performerId": "{{performerId}}"
}

### 201, and **the same trackId as the request above** — this is the thing to watch in a demonstration.
###
### Threads are inferred from who exchanged work with whom, never from what anybody wrote. Two pieces of
### work by the same pair in the same engagement belong together, and nobody was asked to say so.
###
### The key is the role pair *and the performer*, and the performer is the half that gets forgotten: two
### content writers working for the same account manager share a role pair exactly, so keyed on roles
### alone their work lands in one thread and the inferred order reads *blog requested → social requested
### → blog done → social done* — two edges that never happened.
POST {{host}}/api/discovery/nodes
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "messageId": "{{secondMessageId}}",
  "jobId": "{{jobId}}",
  "direction": "COMPLETION",
  "performerId": "{{performerId}}"
}

### 201 with **no trackId at all**, and the emptiness is a real answer rather than a failure.
###
### A status question never opens or joins a thread — invariant I11. It contributes no duration and no
### coverage denominator, so a thread containing one would carry an observation that must never be
### counted, and the exclusion would then have to be remembered by every query downstream instead of
### being impossible. `V65` refuses the combination in the schema rather than trusting anybody to
### remember it.
POST {{host}}/api/discovery/nodes
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "messageId": "{{secondMessageId}}",
  "jobId": "{{jobId}}",
  "direction": "QUERY",
  "performerId": null
}

### 201. Self-directed work nobody requested — which in a business of seven is a great deal of what an
### owner actually does. It still opens a thread, or it would be homeless.
POST {{host}}/api/discovery/nodes
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "messageId": "{{secondMessageId}}",
  "jobId": "{{jobId}}",
  "direction": "STANDALONE",
  "performerId": null
}

### 422 UNKNOWN_JOB. **Refused rather than created silently.**
###
### Creating the engagement here would put work into one nobody opened, and the outer boundary is the
### one piece of structure this zone insists a human supplies. Everything else it infers for free; this
### it will not guess.
POST {{host}}/api/discovery/nodes
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "messageId": "{{messageId}}",
  "jobId": "22222222-2222-2222-2222-222222222222",
  "direction": "REQUEST",
  "performerId": "{{performerId}}"
}

###############################################################################
### The subject chip — pre-selected, pre-guessed, and shown as a guess
###############################################################################

### 200. What the strip offers, and which one it is guessing.
###
### **The chip is a guess and is shown as one.** At most one entry comes back with `guessed: true` —
### the engagement this conversation has already produced work in, failing that the workspace's most
### recently touched open engagement, and failing that **nothing at all**: an empty array, no flag, and
### no pretending.
###
### **Nothing here is applied.** The flag stays a flag and no unit of work is written. Silently linking
### a node to an engagement the person did not see builds a relationship nobody can detect afterwards,
### and *losing a signal beats building a false one* — a missing link is visibly missing, a wrong one
### looks identical to a right one forever.
###
### Copy the guessed `jobId` into `@jobId` above; this is exactly what the strip does.
GET {{host}}/api/discovery/jobs?conversationId={{conversationId}}

### 200. The guess was right, and saying so is worth a write.
###
### Naming the engagement the work already carries records `subject_source = CONFIRMED` and moves
### nothing. *The guess was right* is evidence; silence is not, and a guess nobody looked at and a
### choice somebody made are otherwise the same identifier in the same column.
###
### Copy a `nodeId` from one of the 201s above into `@nodeId`.
PATCH {{host}}/api/discovery/nodes/{{nodeId}}/job
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "jobId": "{{jobId}}"
}

### 200, and **the trackId comes back different** — this is the thing to watch here.
###
### Naming a different engagement records `CORRECTED` and **re-runs the thread inference against the
### new engagement**. A thread is the inner case identifier and never spans two engagements: work left
### pointing at its old thread would sit among work it has nothing to do with, and every duration and
### ordering drawn over that thread afterwards would be wrong with nothing to show for it.
###
### The thread the work left is deleted if it had nothing else in it. A thread with no work in it
### describes nothing, and the next click on the same key would join it.
###
### Open a second engagement first and put its id in `@secondJobId`.
PATCH {{host}}/api/discovery/nodes/{{nodeId}}/job
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "jobId": "{{secondJobId}}"
}

### 422 UNKNOWN_JOB. **Refused rather than created**, exactly as marking is.
###
### The outer boundary is the one piece of structure this zone insists a human supplies, and a chip
### able to conjure an engagement would be the silent creation the other door already refuses.
PATCH {{host}}/api/discovery/nodes/{{nodeId}}/job
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "jobId": "22222222-2222-2222-2222-222222222222"
}

###############################################################################
### The ten-second undo — a full delete, not a tombstone
###############################################################################

### 204. Marked by accident, taken back, and nothing is left behind.
###
### **A full delete.** There is no fact to preserve in a click somebody withdrew before anything else
### happened, and a tombstone would leave a row every later query has to remember to filter — the first
### query that forgets counts a click nobody meant as a unit of work.
###
### It reaches further than the node, and it has to: the evidence goes with it, the thread goes if the
### withdrawn work was its only member, and the engagement goes if this was the click that opened it.
### The message itself is untouched — the node is editable, the message is evidence.
DELETE {{host}}/api/discovery/nodes/{{nodeId}}

### 409 UNDO_WINDOW_CLOSED, once ten seconds have passed. Mark something, wait, and try this.
###
### **The window is measured on the server, against the row's own creation instant.** The countdown in
### the toast is presentation; a refusal that trusted it would let a tab left open overnight delete work
### that has since been threaded, fingerprinted and counted towards a proposal.
###
### **Ten seconds is not a threshold and must never become one** (`DISCOVERY_07` §5). It sits beside
### *one nudge per node* on the list of things that stay constants: it is an interaction affordance, not
### a tuning knob, and a workspace able to set it to a week would have built a delete button without
### ever calling it one.
DELETE {{host}}/api/discovery/nodes/{{nodeId}}

### 422 UNKNOWN_WORK_NODE, for a unit of work that is not there.
###
### Answering 204 would tell you something false about the graph, and a screen that had drifted would go
### on offering undo on work that never existed.
DELETE {{host}}/api/discovery/nodes/33333333-3333-3333-3333-333333333333

###############################################################################
### The permission, and why an employee holds it
###############################################################################

### Sign in as an employee.
POST {{host}}/api/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "{{employee}}",
  "password": "{{password}}"
}

### 201 — an employee may mark. **Every role in the workspace holds `WORK_NODE_MARK`, deliberately.**
###
### This zone has nothing to analyse unless the people doing the work click, and the design's largest
### recorded risk is that they stop. A permission gate on the circle would be a gate on the product's
### whole supply of evidence.
###
### The rule that decides it is in DISCOVERY_02_ACCESS_CONTROL: ask an employee only what they can
### answer by **recognition**, and a manager anything requiring **classification**. *Which engagement is
### this?* is recognition. *Is Aurora a client or a prospect?* is classification. Naming a discovered
### type is the owner's, because no template is ever created without a human — invariant I7.
POST {{host}}/api/discovery/nodes
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "messageId": "{{messageId}}",
  "jobId": "{{jobId}}",
  "direction": "COMPLETION",
  "performerId": null
}

### 422 MESSAGE_NOT_MARKABLE, on a message in a conversation this employee is not in.
###
### **Marking is bounded by what the caller can already see, and the rule is CHAT's — called rather than
### copied.** A second implementation of the same rule is the one that goes stale. This widens nothing;
### it lets somebody act on what was already in front of them.
POST {{host}}/api/discovery/nodes
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "messageId": "{{messageId}}",
  "jobId": "{{jobId}}",
  "direction": "REQUEST",
  "performerId": "{{performerId}}"
}

###############################################################################
### What is not here yet, so its absence is not read as a fault
###############################################################################

### **Nothing in this file draws anything.** There is no canvas read — DISCOVERY-VIEW-CANVAS-01 — so the
### lanes, the cards and the loops that collapse to one box with a counter have nothing to answer them
### yet. Neither has the weekly digest.
###
### **Nothing here discovers a pattern either.** The fingerprint's six components, the clustering that
### groups threads by them, the proposals that reach the owner's weekly and the naming that turns one
### into a word the workspace uses — DISCOVERY-PROPOSE-TYPE-01 and DISCOVERY-NAME-TYPE-01 — are all
### still ahead. Until they ship the graph accumulates and nothing is read out of it, which is the
### honest state rather than a fault.
###
### **And nothing here crosses into the application.** DISCOVERY-FORMALISE-WORK-01 and
### DISCOVERY-COMPOSE-PROCESS-01 are the one thing that crosses zones, one way, under the receiving
### zone's own permissions. There is no request you can write in this file that creates a template, a
### process or a task, and `NoRouteFromDiscoveryToATaskTest` asserts that absence rather than trusting
### it.
###
### **The clocks and closures are here, in the two files beside this one.** Recording what a unit of
### work produced, blocking and resuming it and the one nudge it ever gets are in
### `discovery-progress.http`; ending a thread, pairing a completion with its request and the manager's
### orphan queue are in `discovery-tracks.http`. This file stays the capture story — the circle, the
### chip and the undo — because a single file holding every request in the zone is one nobody reads
### from the top.
